# Host optimization verification record

What was measured, against what, how, and what is *not* signed off. Read
[the feature description](host_opt.md) first.

## r14 positions verification, 2026-11-11

The full package passed 186 CPU tests on CPython 3.11.6, including 92 for PR1.
The admitted positions path checks block-table bounds before any device write
for the step. Verify performs the check before running the original device
path, compares positions, sequence lengths and slot mappings, and logs every
completed comparison.

The r14 CoreX business verify run completed 100/100 requests. Each of the
four Workers recorded 1,950 calls and 1,950 comparisons, with zero mismatches
or fallbacks. The formal window covered 1,690 work steps per Worker.

In the later r14 six-arm run, adding positions to arena changed H+G from
4.004 s to 3.800 s (−5.08%), with S increasing by 170.6 ms. The run used
one service per arm, with PR2 and the !6 sampling options off. See
[the r14 business measurements](../../../../evidence/r14-performance-20261112.md).

Sections below retain earlier validation for the original three PR1 features.

## Naming and admission cleanup verification, 2026-11-05

The r4 container delivery uses `HostOpt` and `VLLM_HOST_OPT*`. CPython 3.11.6
and 3.12.11 each passed 105 CPU tests, comprising 68 for this PR and 37 for
the independent ILU metadata PR. The same bookkeeping abi3 binary built by
3.11 also passed all 105 tests on 3.12. Each PR passed its tests independently.
Dry-run, installation, installed-source checks, normal uninstall and rollback
after an injected build failure passed in private package roots.

Before/after runs used the original `vllmBench_lite.sh`, with an isolated
diagnostic toolkit recording verify counters. Both completed 100/100 requests.
The actual four Workers used CPython 3.11.6, P2P enabled and
`itr_multi_compute_engine=1`. All four features were active. Each rank's last
formal-cleanup snapshot matched the previous implementation exactly.

| Feature | Calls or commits | Fast | Fallback | Verified | Mismatched |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| inputs | 1950 | 1920 | 30 | 1920 | 0 |
| arena | 1950 | 1950 | 0 refusals | 1950 | 0 |
| bookkeeping | 1950 | 1950 | 0 | 1950 | 0 |
| metadata | 2024 | 2024 | 0 | — | — |

These are process-lifetime counters, including warmup and graph preparation.
Metadata exposes handshake and hit counters; its CPU differential tests provide
the numerical comparison. Timing from these diagnostic runs is excluded from
performance acceptance. The performance measurements below retain their
original dates and conditions.

## Baseline

| | |
| --- | --- |
| vLLM | `0.23.0+corex.5.0.0` (an installed distribution, not a git checkout) |
| torch | `2.10.0+corex.5.0.0` |
| NumPy | `1.26.4` |
| Interpreter | CPython 3.11.6 |
| Host | ARM64, 4 accelerators (tensor parallel 4) |
| Scheduling | async on, prefix caching off |
| Graph mode | decode-only capture |
| Historical timing P2P setting | `NCCL_P2P_DISABLE=1`; do not compare with a P2P-enabled baseline |

The measured source was the vendor distribution, which diverges from upstream
v0.23.0 in every file these features touch — `gpu_model_runner.py` alone
differs by 523 lines (7561 → 7813). The changes in this PR are the same
changes, re-expressed against upstream; they are not byte-identical to what was
measured, and the surrounding code is not the same code. Treat the figures
below as the effect of these optimisations on that distribution.

## Workload

`128` input tokens, `128` output tokens, batch size `8`, `100` requests,
`16` warm-up requests, concurrency `8`.

Per rank per run the formal window holds `1692` steps: `1664` decode steps on
the captured graph, and `28` off it (`13` prefill, `13` mixed, `2` cleanup).

